so the way that we have framed the  conversation that we'll be having here  this evening is on the premise that  identity politics has become ever more  influential the left championing  identity is based on sexuality and race  while the right is reasserting the  nation does our desire to belong to one  tribe or another make conflict with  others inevitable could we do away with  tribal allegiances and see ourselves as  part of the same human family or a group  identities based on class race sexuality  or nationality the only effective  vehicles for political change so I'll  introduce each of the panelists first up  Phillip blonde political philosopher  anglican theologian and director of the  respect and also here with a storm  Butler Labour MP representing Brent  central has served as shadow women and  equalities secretary since 2017 and  David Blunkett of course one of the most  respected members of Tony Blair's  cabinet having held many cabinet  positions Education Secretary Home  Secretary and Work and Pensions  secretary and since leaving politics in  May 2015 has worked in academia and for  charities okay well we will hear first  of all from Philipp Lahm what is  happening in the West from America  through through out sort of Western and  Eastern Europe it it's a fragmentation  and a breakdown and the successful  parties are no longer to be understood  on the left-right spectrum they're  actually a mixture of left and right  there are socialists who believe in  leave there are socialists who believe  in remain but leave and remain are now  better predictors of who you're going to  vote for than your past tribal identity  so what's really happening is is  realignment and fragmentation and  largely speaking I want to suggest to  you that the formulation that's taking  place and I think you know you you got  it in one is nationalism on the one hand  and extreme liberalism understood as  identity politics on the other  and I won't suggest you that neither of  them ultimately in my view will work  because we have been governed by extreme  liberalism for really since since the  time of Margaret Thatcher have extreme  economic liberalism on the right extreme  social liberalism on the left and there  a common project and I always think it's  incoherent to hear people on the left's  denouncing neoliberalism economically  but essentially upholding it socially  and and vice versa so what is the  liberal project that is now over the  liberal project is essentially a grand  global project of extreme individualism  and this grand global project have first  appealed to the narcissism of the 1960s  and the genuine injustice --is that the  1960s spoke to but what it creates is a  dispensation economically that only  favors those at the very very top if you  look at the global distribution the  famous elephant graph by Branko  Milanovic he shows that really over the  last 3040 years has been no net gain for  the vast majority of the working  population from globalization and the  gain has been for the working class in  the third world whereas the working  class and increasing the middle class in  the first world has seen no genuine gain  at all you've seen the rise of rampant  insecurity because insecurity stems from  Brampton to individualism and we've  created on the economic level forms of  insecurity that we're actually  unthinkable in the 60s or indeed the 70s  but on the cultural right and culture we  know outcompetes economics in any  assessment of human behavior liberalism  has essentially created a culture that  oppresses majorities that creates  marginalize majorities through identity  politics that are little more than  middle-class power games and attempts by  middle class advocates to both virtue  signal and gain power  within a medium whereby solidarity has  collapsed so don't be surprised that  nationalism is on the way back because  nationalism by those who advocate for it  is the only thing that will care for  them nationalism by it from the  perspective it's of advocates is the  only thing that will care for those who  have lost out whereas liberalism won't  liberalism of of the extreme left has  become an upper-middle class project and  so what we've got is a project where  solidarity are looking after us all has  been captured by nationalism and the  project the right wing project of  extreme individualism is now followed by  both left-wing parties and right-wing  parties that is the new divide that is  the new debate that is where we are so  for me I feel that this is a real sort  of academic debate and not one that I  totally agree with its concept and it's  all about identity politics because you  know politics has always been about  identity me growing up as a as a black  woman will have very different identity  to two other people growing up  themselves and there in their social  environment so I think the main thing is  that politics should make sense and  should be about policies and I think  that's the main basis that we should  start from I mean every human being is  molded by the society that they've grown  up in your molded by how you grew up who  spoke to you the biases that you have  growing up that's that's who we are so  you will fight the different things I  don't think there's anything wrong with  that the problem is that not all  identities are the same so I think as we  start to dismantle all the social  barriers that contribute to the  disparities in society that's when  people start saying oh my god identity  politics is bad because you are you are  taken away my privilege all of a sudden  and you want to take some of my  privilege away and I don't like it the  whole term identity poly  it just means we are doing politics and  I need to I need to fight for things  that I believe in because that's where  my passion is and if you want to call it  identity politics call it that okay well  um in March I was blagged into doing  something for comic relief with David  Williams and I had to stand up and swing  left woods and say ladies I still swing  to the left and I still swing to the  left and I'm tribal in political terms  tribalism in this sense meaning having a  real sense of identity with the people  that I've worked with that I've grown up  with that I share values with and even  when we are in conflict with each other  about ideas or direction we still have a  sense of that common purpose so there  can be tribalism that actually is a glue  that holds people together in a positive  sense and there can be identity and  nationalism which is narrow and  exclusive and anything but what in the  jargon would be called civic Republic  ism so I believe in mutuality and  reciprocity of trying to put the glue  back in so that people do feel a sense  of belonging a sense that they have some  identity with the people around them and  that they will care about them as the as  you would with an extended family and  that's not to do with borders or  language or faith or even culture it's  to do with how people see each other and  work with each other I think that the  danger at the moment and Phillip has  touched on it and I heard dawn earlier  mentioning it before we came in is the  idea that our identity is exclusive to  particular aspects of our life or  background identity of gender or sexual  orientation or faith or of disability  seems to me to be extremely dangerous  because it separates out by people's  very  specific elements within them rather  than that they have that common  citizenship and coming together and  maybe we'd like to debate that because I  find it really disturbing that people  want to categorize by things that should  not separate us  I've therefore in favor of patriotism in  the George Orwell sense rather than  nationalism I'm in favor of putting the  glue back in in building from the  community think global act local so that  people do feel that they've got  something in common and finding things  that unites rather than dividers and  doing that builds a different sort of  sense of identity and belonging and a  different kind of tribalism to what we  see at the moment which deliberately  seeks to separate deliberately plays on  people's worst instincts deliberately  intends to build up resentment and  dislike an often hatred about other  people and in the end that has to mean  that we get people to feel that they've  got something real in common rather than  that what divides them is the thing that  identifies and separates and creates  that identity all of our panelists  Philipp Lahm Don Butler David Blunkett  [Applause]
 

